  "body": "Found while writing the #926 per-post script suites.\n\n**What is wrong.** `post --machine <file.mch>` (the command-line post, `crates/post/src/bin/post.rs`) hands the script `post::machine::script_props` of the raw file. `script_props` reads the static grammar (`kinematics.static.rotaries`, `kinematics.static.head.spindle.max`, `...toolStation.coolants`, `rapidRate`, ...). Every shipped machine in `assets/machines/` is a vendor kinematics tree (`kinematics.default.parts`), which the app first parses (`machine::parse_mch`) and then mirrors (`post::machine::mirror`) into that grammar. The binary parses the file only for its inverted-axis refusal and then drops the parsed machine, so the script sees no rotaries, no spindle limit, no coolant list, no rates.\n\nSeen: `post --machine assets/machines/tilthead.mch fanuc.cps <tilted stream>` refuses \"tilted move on a machine without a tilting rotary\" although the file states A and B; posting the same stream with `mirror(parse_mch(..))` works (the #926 suites do that as a workaround, see `crates/post/tests/recorded/mod.rs`).\n\nWhy P1: the CLI posts a different program for the same machine than the app does (missing spindle cap, coolant codes and rotaries; refusals where the app posts).\n\nDone means: the binary posts with the mirrored machine (keeping the raw file for `invert::apply`), so `post --machine <vendor .mch>` gives the app's program; pinned by a CLI test on `tilthead.mch`; the #926 runner then passes the file straight through.\n",
